Hi TaiJu,

Thanks for the KIP.

JY0: Since the controller now holds brokers' static configs, are there =
any plans to=20
allow the DescribeConfigs API to serve static config queries directly =
from the=20
controller side?

JY1: There's a typo in the KIP where RegisterBrokerRecord is misspelled =
as=20
BrokerRegerationRecord.

JY2: Since the filtering rule is "include configs that have a =
Validator," what happens=20
when a config gains a validator in a newer version?=20
For example, suppose some.config has no validator in 4.X but gains one =
in 4.X+1.=20
4.X brokers would not report it (since no validator exists). As a =
result, the 4.X+1 controller=20
would have only a partial view. This can be problematic for downstream =
KIP-1294, where the=20
controller must verify that all brokers satisfy a constraint before =
allowing a MetadataVersion=20
upgrade. If some brokers never report the config, the controller may =
fail to validate those=20
configs correctly.

Best Regards,
Jiunn-Yang


> TaiJu Wu <tjwu1217@gmail.com> =E6=96=BC 2026=E5=B9=B45=E6=9C=882=E6=97=A5=
 =E4=B8=8B=E5=8D=882:01 =E5=AF=AB=E9=81=93=EF=BC=9A
>=20
> Hi Muralidhar Basani,
>=20
> Thanks for your feedback.
>=20
> mb-0:
>  Importance and "should be sent to the controller" are two unrelated
> concerns. A HIGH-importance label means operators
>  should pay attention to a value; it has nothing to do with whether =
the
> controller needs to know it. The criterion for
>  inclusion is whether the controller can validate the value =E2=80=94 =
i.e.,
> whether the config has a validator. A config
>  without a validator gives the controller nothing to enforce, so =
sending
> its value would just bloat the metadata log
>  with data the controller cannot act on.
>=20
>  Under this rule, your three examples are internally consistent:
>=20
>  - replica.fetch.min.bytes =E2=80=94 no validator =E2=86=92 excluded
>  - replica.fetch.max.bytes =E2=80=94 has atLeast(0) validator =E2=86=92 =
included
>  - replica.lag.time.max.ms =E2=80=94 no validator =E2=86=92 excluded
>=20
>  The cases that look surprising =E2=80=94 HIGH-importance configs that =
lack
> validators =E2=80=94 are a Kafka codebase issue: those
>  configs should have validators added, after which this KIP picks them =
up
> automatically with no further code change.
>  That cleanup is independent of this KIP.
>=20
>=20
> mb-1:
> The inclusion rule should track the actual signal we care about: "the
> controller can run the same validator the broker
>  runs." Validator presence captures that signal directly; an opt-in =
flag
> does not. With explicit opt-in, the
>  wire-level set drifts away from that signal =E2=80=94 configs that =
gain a
> validator stay excluded until someone remembers to
>  flip a flag, and configs without validators can be opted in despite =
the
> controller having nothing to enforce on them.
>  The maintenance overhead of tagging every config is a real cost on =
top,
> but the bigger issue is the misalignment.
>=20
>  Your underlying concern about silent inclusion when a future PR adds =
a
> validator to an ssl.* config is
>  legitimate. I'll add a two-state policy on ConfigKey:
>=20
>  enum ControllerReportPolicy { DEFAULT, NEVER }
>=20
>  NEVER guarantees the config is never reported, regardless of =
validator
> presence or future filter changes. Plus a
>  defineSecurity(...) overload that fixes the policy at NEVER:
>=20
>  .defineSecurity(SslConfigs.SSL_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD_CONFIG, PASSWORD, =
null,
>                  MEDIUM, SslConfigs.SSL_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD_DOC)
>=20
>  All SSL/SASL/PASSWORD entries in BrokerSecurityConfigs move to
> defineSecurity, including ssl.client.auth.
>=20
> mb-2:
>  Thanks for catching this, I will update KIP to according mb-1, hope =
it
> can answer this question.
>=20
> Best regards,
> TaiJuWu
>=20
>=20
> Muralidhar Basani via dev <dev@kafka.apache.org> =E6=96=BC =
2026=E5=B9=B45=E6=9C=881=E6=97=A5=E9=80=B1=E4=BA=94 =E4=B8=8A=E5=8D=881:08=
=E5=AF=AB=E9=81=93=EF=BC=9A
>=20
>> Hi TaiJuWu,
>>=20
>> Thanks for the kip.  I have a few thoughts below.
>>=20
>> mb-0 : I have gone through the rejected alternatives. If only configs =
which
>> have validator are included, there are several configs which don't =
have a
>> validator, true.
>>=20
>> For ex :
>> replica.fetch.min.bytes has no validator (excluded)
>> replica.fetch.max.bytes has a validator (included)
>> replica.lag.time.max.ms has no validator, (excluded but HIGH =
importance)
>>=20
>> There are many examples like these. thinking what exactly controller =
needs
>> to check or what could be the deciding factor for the config to be =
part of
>> BrokerRegistrationRecord?
>>=20
>> mb-1 : If an author today adds a validator to an existing config, it
>> silently ends up in controller. How about an opt-in flag
>> (sendToController)?
>> Many configs like ssl.* and sasl.* have no validator and they are =
excluded
>> which is good. But if a future pr changes them like adding =
NonEmptyString,
>> will end up in controller.
>>=20
>> mb-2 : Config like SSL_CLIENT_AUTH_CONFIG =3D "ssl.client.auth" have =
a
>> validator. It is a BrokerSecurityConfig. I am not sure if it's =
considered
>> into the push record? But kip mentions ... "this naturally excludes =
most
>> security/SSL/SASL configs.". Thought of clarifying.
>>=20
>> Thanks,
>> Murali
>>=20
>> On Thu, Apr 30, 2026 at 4:48=E2=80=AFPM TaiJu Wu <tjwu1217@gmail.com> =
wrote:
>>=20
