Hi Mickael,

> MM3: I think setting didMetadataChange to true would make things simpler.
We don't really have a way to perform downgrade checks at the moment, so
you'd need to introduce a new mechanism.

re MM3: Okay. I have updated the KIP.

> When you parse "::ffff:192.168.1.1" with InetAddress.parse(), it returns
an Inet4Address instance. So you should be able to check it against an IPv4
CIDR.

re MM4:

What do you mean by InetAddress.parse()? There is no such method in the
java.net.InetAddress. If you mean, InetAddress.getByName() then
yes getByName("::ffff:192.168.1.1") returns an Inet4Address and I
eventually can check it.

> MM5: Following from MM4, what happens in the opposite scenario, an ACL
with "::ffff:192.168.1.1/24" and a client with an ipv6 address?

re MM5:

::ffff:192.168.1.1/24 is rejected at ACL creation time with an
InvalidRequestException. My method parseCidrAddress strips the prefix and
calls InetAddress.getByName("::ffff:192.168.1.1"), which the JVM normalizes
to a plain Inet4Address. So isIpv6("192.168.1.1") returns false, and we
call new SubnetUtils("::ffff:192.168.1.1/24"), which rejects it because
::ffff: is not valid IPv4 notation.

The deprecated IPv4-compatible form (::192.168.1.1/24) behaves differently.
The JVM does not normalize it and returns an Inet6Address
(0:0:0:0:0:0:c0a8:101), so it passes validation as a regular IPv6 CIDR. A
/24 on a 128-bit IPv6 address masks the top 24 bits, meaning ::
192.168.1.0/24 covers 0:: through 0:ff:ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff an
enormous and almost certainly unintended range. It would match an IPv6
client where the first byte is 0x00 (i.e., 0:0:: through 0:ff:ffff:...),
but would not match a plain IPv4 client (e.g., 192.168.1.5) since that goes
through SubnetUtils, not SubnetUtils6.

Cheers,
Maros


