Hi Murali,

On Tue, May 5, 2026, at 12:27 PM, Muralidhar Basani via dev wrote:
> Hi Kirk,
>=20
> Thanks for clarifying.
>=20
> > mb-5 : Regarding configs.push.allowed.keys, would it help if there i=
s a
> > deny list which can override allow list? or to avoid any sensitive k=
eys
> > just in case.
>=20
> >>  Can you provide a use case that would require that?
>=20
> Since configs.push.allowed.keys is an exact-match list, if an org has =
keys
> like org.batching.strategy, org.auth.token, it could be easily overloo=
ked
> and sensitive keys can end up there, as they don't get filtered with
> default ssl.*.

I guess the question is: do we allow custom keys in configs.push.allowed=
.keys? If yes, then there's a chance that the user could specify configs=
 which are unknowingly (to the client) sensitive. If no, then we block t=
hat possibility, but we prevent all custom configuration, which could be=
 very limiting.

> In the same context, if wildcards are allowed or being planned, then t=
he
> risk becomes high and denylist can become more important.

There are no plans as of this time for wildcards.

Thanks,
Kirk

