Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

Hi Matthias,

On Tue, May 12, 2026, at 10:39 PM, Matthias J. Sax wrote:
> Thanks for the KIP Kirk,
>=20
> I did not find time yet, to read the KIP in full detail, but I would=20
> like to follow up on Mickael's question about Streams and Connect. Als=
o,=20
> the relationship to KIP-1313 (and KIP-714, KIP-1076) is something I=20
> would like to ask about.
>=20
> Starting with Connect, I am frankly not sure if I agree with Mickael?=20
> While Connect is a "client" from a broker POV, Connect is still its ow=
n=20
> cluster. So I would assume that a Connect cluster is run by the same=20
> operator that also runs the broker cluster, and users would submit the=
ir=20
> Connectors into the cluster. Thus, I am wondering, if any such config=20
> monitoring should go into the Connect framework directly? -- Sending=20
> configs to the brokers is the only way (and to some extend a workaroun=
d=20
> IMHO) to do this monitoring for "actual clients"=20
> (producer/consumer/streams), but Connect might be different, and I wou=
ld=20
> assume it might actually be much simpler to do the monitoring directly=20
> inside the Connect framework?
>=20
>=20
> I think it's great the we include Streams in the KIP right away. For=20
> KIP-714, this did not happen, and we needed to do KIP-1076 as a follow=20
> up. However, currently the KIP only mentioned high level that Streams=20
> would get a new config to enable/disable the config push. It's not cle=
ar=20
> how this push would actually happen? Atm, Streams treats the clients=20
> more or less as black-boxes (with the exception of KIP-1071...). For=20
> KIP-714, we extended the clients API (via KIP-1076) allowing to push=20
> custom metric via the underlying clients. For metrics collection, we=20
> actually use a mix of consumer and admin client inside Streams to send=20
> the different metrics. The disadvantage is still, that the Streams=20
> metrics use the same clientInstanceId as the consumer/admin client we=20
> are piggy-bagging on. This might be fine for metrics: the subscription=20
> is dynamic, and thus any plugin would accumulate all reported values.=20
> For collecting configs, it's a single hand-shake between the client an=
d=20
> the broker though, so it seems this different.
>=20
> While we could maybe do a similar thing as for KIP-714/KIP-1076, we=20
> would still need a public API (on the admin client) to allow Streams t=
o=20
> hand its own configs to the admin client for a push -- but if the admi=
n=20
> client used its own clientInstanceId, we would get two=20
> `PushConfigRequest` on the same connection what seems to go against th=
e=20
> design of the KIP. I am also worried to "confuse" the broker side=20
> plugin, and a second on the same clientInstanceId would overwrite the=20
> previous one? -- Or, as an alternative we would need to give the Strea=
ms=20
> config to the admin client before it connects to the broker, what does=20
> also seem to be tricky to get right. Also, we get a config mix on the=20
> same clientInstanceId what does not sound ideal either.

I'd prefer that the Kafka Streams application not reuse either the admin=
 client or consumer.

Both ClientUtils and NetworkClient live in o.a.k.clients, so they are av=
ailable to use by either Kafka Streams or Connect. Per the KIP design, N=
etworkClient issues the ApiVersions and PushConfig RPCs internally. The =
idea is that Kafka Streams or Connect would create a NetworkClient via C=
lientUtils, leave it open until it's verified the PushConfig attempt was=
 made (failure or success), then close it.

> With regard to KIP-1313, I was actually thinking if we should introduc=
e=20
> a "logical client" concept, and allow a "logical client" like Streams=20
> (ie, doesn't open its own network connection), to still get it's own=20
> clientInstanceId? If we follow KIP-1313 and clients generate their own=20
> UUIDs, it would be easy for Streams to also generate its own=20
> clientInstanceId. This might allow us much more easily to send two=20
> `PushConfigRequest` with two different `clientInstanceIds`. -- Btw: I=20
> would believe that other "logical clients / thrid-party frameworks" li=
ke=20
> Apache Flink or Apache Spark, might also benefit?

I need to add a logicalClientInstanceId to the PushConfig RPC. It's null=
 except in cases like Kafka Streams, where it would provide its client i=
nstance ID when the embedded client issues its PushConfig RPC.

Thanks,
Kirk

>=20
> Furthermore, if we also change KIP-714 and let the client generate its=20
> UUID for metrics push, it would be good to use the same UUID for both=20
> pushing Streams metrics, and the Streams config. KIP-1331 from Lucas,=20
> could also benefit from a unique logical clienstInstanceId for Streams=
.=20
> Of course, but this is more a KIP-1313 discussion, if we go this route=
,=20
> we might need a few more changes. But if we get a better overall=20
> solution, and can unify different concepts it seems to be worth it.
>=20
>=20
> -Matthias
>=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
> On 5/12/26 2:18 PM, Apoorv Mittal wrote:
> > Hi Kirk,
> > Thanks for the KIP. I have some questions:
> >=20
> > AM1. The KIP in the request-response flow diagram mentions, "Validate
> > sensitive configs were excluded". Can you please help me understand =
what
> > happens if the sensitive configs are somehow determined by
> > ClientConfigPolicy? Has the client been notified, or what's the expe=
cted
> > behaviour next?
> >=20
> > AM2. The KIP defines default configuration keys for which data will =
be
> > transmitted. Also there is a client configuration that can override =
which
> > keys to send. If a new client configuration is added in the future, =
we need
> > to decide whether to include it in the default set, correct? I am
> > confirming to determine whether a deny list instead of an allow list=
 would
> > be helpful, however, the allow list seems less error-prone when
> > transmitting client-related configurations.
> >=20
> > AM3. Unlike KIP-714 where the broker determines which metrics the cl=
ient
> > receives, and the subscription can change dynamically for the client=
. This
> > KIP has a default set present in the client; therefore, should we me=
ntion
> > why some configs like connections.max.idle.ms, max.block.ms,
> > max.request.size,metadata.max.age.ms, etc. are not in the default se=
t for
> > Java producer? Similarly, this applies to the Consumer and Share Con=
sumer
> > segments. Expecting clients to define all configuration keys for
> > transmission in properties is cumbersome; therefore, I suggest using=
 a
> > broader default list.
> >=20
> > AM4. The KIP defines new exception ClientConfigTooLargeException whe=
n the
> > configuration size is exceeded. What will the client do when this ex=
