Hi Matthias,
Thanks for driving this. I'm a bit less concerned about it than you, but stro=
nger rules would eliminate the typical release schedule alignment discussions=
 we've seen in all of the recent releases.

I would go for 3 releases of equal lengths in March, July and November. I fav=
our the 3+2 schedule.

Thanks,
Andrew

On 2026/05/27 05:43:04 "Matthias J. Sax" wrote:
> Hello,
>=20
> I would like to start a discussion about our time-based release=20
> schedule. We have a discussion (that I personally find a little bit=20
> annoying and tedious) about release plan dates on every single release=20
> (the 4.4.0 discussion, is just another example). And we still often end=20
> up with none-optimal time-frames (4.4 might also be an example here --=20
> not by itself, but the impact on 4.5 release timeline -- cf my latest=20
> reply on the other thread).
>=20
> Given that we actually plan to have time-based releases, I think we=20
> should agree on a more fixed schedule, which allows us to project all=20
> release dates (including KIP/feature/code freeze) into the future and=20
> just be done with it. -- And even if we need an exception from the rule,=20
> we can easily get back on track on future release afterwards.
>=20
> I was also looking into historic data, and while we overall don't do too=20
> bad to stay on track for 3 release per year, we had to pay the price of=20
> a few short release cycles to "shift back" (the previous 4.3 release is=20
> a good example of it), while we still did shift somewhat forward=20
> overall. These short releases cause even more pain IMHO.
>=20
> To avoid problems with the holiday season end of the year, I would like=20
> to propose the following fixed release cadence (this is not my personal=20
> favorite, but let's start here):
>=20
> 1. KIP freeze is the second Wednesday of January, May, and September.
> 2. Feature freeze =3D KIP freeze + 3 weeks.
> 3. Code freeze =3D Feature freeze + 3 weeks.
> 4. Release target =3D Code freeze + 4 weeks.
>=20
> While we never officially formalized the 3+3 weeks between KIP/feature=20
> and feature/code freeze dates (and we used shorter time windows in the=20
> past, ie, 2+2 weeks), 3+3 established itself since 4.0 release -- I am=20
> totally open for other time frames, but believe we should not go shorter=20
> than 2+2 weeks. I would also not make them longer than 3+3, as it would=20
> just give us the subjective impression that releases are closer to each=20
> other.
>=20
> A 3+2 schedule would actually be my personal preference. For KIPs,=20
> giving only 2 weeks to complete them is tight, even for simple KIPs. And=20
> for bug-fixes, I would believe that 2 weeks are enough. Making it=20
> overall 5 week instead of 6, also helps to keep releases 1 week "further=20
> apart" from each other (not really, but we know how reality works, given=20
> our subjective nature).
>=20
> The above schedule implies that we would have target release dates late=20
> March, July, and November. Even if we need more than 4 weeks after code=20
> freeze, we still have some head room in the November release before the=20
> holidays (we might hit Thanksgiving, but I think that's ok). For the KIP=20
> freeze in January, it should also give enough time after the holidays to=20
> complete KIPs and get them accepted (w/o being too close to the=20
> holidays, eg, "first Wednesday in January" would effectively mean, that=20
> KIPs must get voted before the holiday break).
>=20
> If we want to take the holiday season into account even more, we could=20
> also make the timeline between "release 3" and "release 1 of the=20
> following year" two weeks longer, and the other two timelines 1 week=20
> shorter. This would give us the following KIP freeze deadlines:
>=20
>   - third Wednesday of January
>   - second Wednesday of May
>   - first Wednesday of September
>=20
> It also implies, that Thanksgiving would be less of an issue for=20
> "release 3". This schedule is what I would personally prefer, instead of=20
> an 100% equal distribution of our 52 weeks across all three releases.
>=20
>=20
>=20
> For the upcoming 4.4 release, we should make it a 5-month cycle=20
> targeting October instead of September, and we would also have a=20
> slightly longer cycle for 4.6, which we should only start in January.=20
> However, starting with 4.6 we would enter the above proposed fixed=20
> timeline, putting us into a much better position going forward IMHO.
>=20
>=20
> Looking forward to your thoughts on this idea.
>=20
>=20
> -Matthias
>=20
>=20
>=20

