> about this below).
>=20
>=20
> I was also a little bit concerned about the "convention to align request=20
> header clientInstanceId to request body fields". The KIP only say that=20
> bumping request versions to avoid this question would be a=20
> "protocol-compatibility nightmare". Digging into it a little bit, I=20
> don't see why it would be a huge problem for KIP-714 RPCs, and I am=20
> wondering if it would still be worth to do the version bump for these=20
> RPCs? Maybe Andrew can elaborate?
>=20
> However, after some digging I learned that KIP-848/1071 (including=20
> classic group RPCs) actually encoded the `memberId` as `string` type at=20
> protocol level. So while it's an UUID client side, it's not really=20
> broker side. So there is not much to be aligned? It's of course not a=20
> bad idea to generate a single UUID and use for both, but from a protocol=20
> level it seems that bumping these RPCs versions to remove the `string`=20
> fields, and replace with request header `clientInstanceId` field might=20
> not be the right thing to do to begin with. -- So while I agree to the=20
> "nightmare" assessment, it would still be good if the KIP could add more=20
> context why it would be a nightmare.
>=20
>=20
>=20
> For KIP-714/1076, the underlying idea of a logical client instance ID=20
> was to know which metrics belong to the physical client vs logical=20
> client. However, the challenge is that a single PushTelemetryRequest may=20
> contain both "native" client metrics as well as user-registered metrics.=20
> Thus, adding a new `logicalClientInstanceId` field at the request level=20
> does not really help (neither does the proposed prefix). I don't think=20
> it would be a good idea to send multiple smaller requests (with=20
> different IDs) to keep "native" and "custom" metrics separated. -- It=20
> was also not a problem yet, to only have the single `clientInstanceId`,=20
> so maybe there is no problem to be solved to begin with? -- As an=20
> alternative, I was thinking if it could make sense to instead encode a=20
> logical client instance id (=3D=3D KS ProcessId) as metric tag (either at K=
S=20
> level directly, or by adding it dynamically before sending to the=20
> broker). Some KS metrics already have the `ProcessId` as tag. But it=20
> seems we are heading into a quite independent discussion now, that has=20
> not much to do with KIP-1313 any longer, so I think we might want to=20
> defer it?
>=20
>=20
> The only open question is KIP-1324, for which we indeed cannot use the=20
> same `clientInstanceId` for eg, consumer and KS configs. But the KIP is=20
> still under discussion, so maybe we can find some other solution (there=20
> is the idea to hijack the Admin's network connection...).
>=20
>=20
> Side note: KIP-1331 did drop the `UUID` for the newly added RPCs and we=20
> don't need to worry about it any longer.
>=20
>=20
>=20
> So long story short. I am still wondering if bumping request versions of=20
> `GetTelemetrySubscriptionRequest/Response` and `PushTelemetryRequest`=20
> could make sense to simply what we try to do with KIP-1313. The=20
> currently propose protocol for old/new broker/client compatibility seems=20
> to be overly (and unnecessarily) complex? If we would bump the RPC=20
> versiona, it would be much simpler:
>=20
>   - if either broker or client is on older version; nothing changes; v0=20
> won't include the new request headers `clientInstanceId` field, and the=20
> broker would still assign a `clientInstanceId` if it receive a ZERO
>=20
>   - only if both broker and client support the new RPC version, the=20
> `clientInstanceId` is added to the request header, and we can drop the=20
> `clientInstanceId` field from both=20
> GetTelemetrySubscriptionRequest/Response body, and maybe also from=20
> PushTelemetryRequest body?
>=20
> I would strongly prefer this simplification. These changes have nothing=20
> to do with "logical client instance ID", so should be part of KIP-1313=20
> (if we agree to do this this way).
>=20
>=20
> -Matthias
