Matthias, you are definitely a master of scheduling! I'm +100 to your plans.

Also, it is absolutely helpful to write down the exact release dates for the =
next few years at once. It will ease our anxiety a lot! I'd be more than happ=
y to update the wiki with these dates once we all agree on this new schedule.=
 For example:

Version	KIP Freeze	Feature Freeze	Code Freeze	Target Release
4.4.0	Jun 17, 2026	Jul 8, 2026	Jul 22, 2026	Aug 19, 2026
4.5.0	Oct 7, 2026	Oct 28, 2026	Nov 11, 2026	Dec 9, 2026
4.6.0	Mar 3, 2027	Mar 24, 2027	Apr 7, 2027	May 5, 2027
4.7.0	Jun 23, 2027	Jul 14, 2027	Jul 28, 2027	Aug 25, 2027
4.8.0	Oct 13, 2027	Nov 3, 2027	Nov 17, 2027	Dec 15, 2027
4.9.0	Mar 1, 2028	Mar 22, 2028	Apr 5, 2028	May 3, 2028
5.0.0	Jun 21, 2028	Jul 12, 2028	Jul 26, 2028	Aug 23, 2028
5.1.0	Oct 11, 2028	Nov 1, 2028	Nov 15, 2028	Dec 13, 2028

Furthermore, let's do our best to avoid fixing release blockers under the Chr=
istmas tree :)

On 2026/05/28 21:22:51 "Matthias J. Sax" wrote:
> Thanks for the input.
>=20
> Overall, it seems that 3+2 weeks for feature freeze / code freeze=20
> windows gets a lot of support.
>=20
> However, the idea of dividing the release windows not into 3 equally=20
> long junks, but be more flexible to account for holiday seasons, seems=20
> not to get a lot of support? Not sure why... I personally think it would=20
> be good to take it into account, and I also think that Chia-Ping raises=20
> a very good point.
>=20
>=20
>=20
> @Chia-Ping: Other holidays are a great point. Thanks for raising it. I=20
> did actually briefly think about it, but it's difficult, and thus I just=20
> started with something simple as proposal to kick off a discussion.
>=20
> I think, Diwali is short enough to not make it a concern (similar to=20
> Thanksgiving)?
>=20
> For Lunar New Year, I agree that it would be good to plan for it.=20
> However, it seems it's shifting quite a lot year-over-year what makes it=20
> very difficult (w/o a huge window) to include?
>=20
> Overall, a 5 / 3.5 / 3.5 schedule seems to be quite unbalanced (this=20
> would be 22 / 15 / 15 weeks per release? And two very short 3.5 month=20
> releases does not sound very appealing?
>=20
>=20
> I was playing with it a little bit more. Overall we have 52 weeks, so=20
> roughly 17.3 weeks per release. So we can naturally split it into=20
> 17/17/18 using the additional week for the holidays. But that's not good=20
> enough to take both Christmas and Lunar New Year into account. However,=20
> with a 16/16/20, we could make it work I believe, even if it does cut it=20
> tight sometimes... To be able to phrase a "simple rule", we can use ISO=20
> weeks:
>=20
> KIP freeze would be Wednesday's, weeks 9 / 25 / 41, with feature freeze=20
> +3 weeks, and code freeze +2 weeks.
>=20
> Ie, we put the first KIP freeze after LNY with plenty of buffer for most=20
> years:
>=20
